To ensure that multiple date pickers function correctly after cloning, you should avoid using IDs for your input fields, as IDs must be unique within a document. Instead, use a common class for the date pickers. For example, you can change your input field to:

```html
<input type="text" class="datepicker" />
```

Then, in your document ready function, initialize all date pickers with that class:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function() {
    $('.datepicker').datepicker();
});
```

When you clone the date picker, ensure to call the datepicker initialization on the newly cloned element as well. This way, all elements with the class `.datepicker` will have the datepicker functionality applied to them. 

This approach was successfully used in a similar scenario where a user replaced IDs with a common class to allow multiple date pickers to work properly after cloning [duplicating jQuery datepicker](34790).

