To create a custom warning for unsaved changes before a user leaves a webpage, you can utilize the `onbeforeunload` event in JavaScript. However, note that while you can set a custom message, the default dialog that appears cannot be fully overridden or replaced with a custom modal dialog due to browser restrictions.

Here’s a basic implementation to get you started:

1. **Track the "dirty" state of your form**: This can be done by comparing the current values of the form elements to their default values.

2. **Set up the `onbeforeunload` event**: This will trigger a warning when the user attempts to leave the page.

Here’s a sample implementation:

```javascript
function formIsDirty(form) {
    for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) {
        var element = form.elements[i];
        var type = element.type;
        if (type === "checkbox" || type === "radio") {
            if (element.checked !== element.defaultChecked) {
                return true;
            }
        } else if (type === "hidden" || type === "password" || type === "text" || type === "textarea") {
            if (element.value !== element.defaultValue) {
                return true;
            }
        } else if (type === "select-one" || type === "select-multiple") {
            for (var j = 0; j < element.options.length; j++) {
                if (element.options[j].selected !== element.options[j].defaultSelected) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return false;
}

window.onbeforeunload = function(e) {
    var form = document.forms["yourFormName"]; // Replace with your form name
    if (formIsDirty(form)) {
        var message = "You have unsaved changes.";
        e = e || window.event;
        if (e) {
            e.returnValue = message; // For IE and Firefox
        }
        return message; // For other browsers
    }
};
```

### Important Notes:
- This code snippet checks if the form is "dirty" and displays a warning message when the user tries to leave the page.
- You cannot customize the default dialog completely; the message you set will be appended to the browser's own warning text, as specified in the documentation [How can I override the OnBeforeUnload dialog and replace it with my own?](Post ID: 276660).
- Ensure you test this functionality across different browsers, as behavior can vary.

This should help you implement a mechanism to warn users about unsaved changes effectively.

