To stop fetching external definitions (like DTDs) while processing an XML document using Perl, you can utilize the `XML::Parser` module's option to not use LWP for resolving external entities. You can create an `XML::Parser` object with the `NoLWP` option set to `1`, and then pass this object to `XML::XPath`. Here’s an example of how to do this:

```perl
use XML::XPath;
use XML::Parser;

my $p = XML::Parser->new( NoLWP => 1 );
my $xp = XML::XPath->new( parser => $p, filename => "a.xhtml" );
```

This approach prevents the parser from fetching external DTDs, which can significantly speed up the XML processing. However, note that using this method may result in the loss of some entities, as the parser will not resolve them except for numerical ones and a few default ones like `>`, `<`, `&`, `'`, and `"`. 

For better performance and features, consider using `XML::LibXML`, which has a similar interface to `XML::XPath` and is more powerful and actively maintained [How can I prevent XML::XPath from fetching a DTD while processing an XML file?](303510).

To create a query string from a list of field names in Java, you can use a method similar to the `http_build_query` function in PHP. You can iterate over a map containing your field names and their corresponding values, encoding them appropriately. Here’s a sample implementation:

```java
import java.io.UnsupportedEncodingException;
import java.net.URLEncoder;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Map.Entry;

public String toQueryString(Map<String, String> data) throws UnsupportedEncodingException {
    StringBuilder queryString = new StringBuilder();
    for (Entry<String, String> pair : data.entrySet()) {
        queryString.append(URLEncoder.encode(pair.getKey(), "UTF-8") + "=");
        queryString.append(URLEncoder.encode(pair.getValue(), "UTF-8") + "&");
    }
    if (queryString.length() > 0) {
        queryString.deleteCharAt(queryString.length() - 1); // Remove the trailing '&'
    }
    return queryString.toString();
}
```

This code constructs a query string by encoding each key-value pair from the map. Make sure to handle `UnsupportedEncodingException` that may arise from the `URLEncoder.encode` method. 

For a more structured approach, you could also look into using a class like `QueryStringBuilder`, as mentioned in the context, which abstracts this functionality and provides a cleaner way to build query strings [Is there any Java equivalent of PHP's http_build_query function?](636770).
